Tournament selection samples ten candidates and returns the fittest. Stronger candidates are favored without making every parent the global best. The population must contain at least ten individuals.

# Selection: Tournament selection
def selection(population):
    return max(random.sample(population, 10), key=fitness)

Process Pending Arcs
The loop calls revise on one directed arc. If a domain shrinks, incoming arcs from other neighbors are queued again because values they previously supported may now have lost their support.
An empty domain proves inconsistency and returns False. An empty queue means no listed arc currently needs revision, so the function returns True. It does not mean all domains are singletons.
The next cell defines revise. Python resolves that name when ac3 is called, so define all helpers before running solve.

def ac3(constraints: List[Tuple[str, str, int]], domains: dict):
    queue = deque(constraints)
    while queue:
        (xi, xj, value) = queue.popleft()
        if revise(domains, xi, xj, value):
            print(f'queue: {queue}')
            print_domains(domains)            
            if not domains[xi]:
                return False
            for xk, xl, this_value in constraints:
                if xl == xi and xk != xj:
                    # print the constraints that will be added to the queue
                    print(f'adding {xk}+{xl}={this_value}')
                    queue.append((xk, xi, this_value))
    return True

