Define Mean Squared Error
Mean squared error (MSE) averages squared prediction errors. We require matching prediction and target shapes to avoid accidental broadcasting. For this one-example, one-output case, L=(\hat y-y)^2=(2-4)^2=4.
There is no factor of one half, matching the slides. For m averaged scalar output entries, \partial L/\partial\hat y_i=2(\hat y_i-y_i)/m. prediction.numel() counts those entries.

def mse_loss(prediction, target):
    if prediction.shape != target.shape:
        raise ValueError("Prediction and target shapes must match")
    return (prediction - target).square().mean()

